friction heating do not perform this function. Develop a general, parameterized exposure-assessment and occupancy-trial operation, reusing existing observation, navigation and thermal-update code. The feasible initial version tests actual occupied locations; it does not create shelter or infer canopy from an object's name or tree icon. Incomplete thermal mechanisms belong in the current-conversation development handoff, not a permanent refusal.\\\",\\\"specification\\\":\\\"General operation: Assess environmental thermal exposure. Modes: inspect, sample, compare, inspect-progress, resume and cancel. Arguments: an optional exact visible targetId or visible position for inspection; an optional exact personal baseline-record ID; sampleRounds, an integer 1–3, default 1. Any applicable living actor may learn it. Inspection of a remote visible target reports only observable geometry and represented protection, not unmeasured temperatures or hidden resources. Sampling is restricted to the actor's actual occupied location. Moving to a candidate location is a separate independently chosen navigate action, with its existing collision checks, speed 0.8 and distance-times-2 energy cost increased by carried mass. Do not duplicate or bypass kernel.navigate.\\\\nCosts and prerequisites: study takes 2 ordinary rounds at 0.2 energy per round. inspect and inspect-progress take 1 round and 0.2 energy; compare takes 1 round and 0.2 energy; sample takes 0.2 energy per completed ordinary round, for 1–3 rounds. No material, water, tool, fuel or equipment is supplied or consumed. Work requires life, sufficient energy and observable/reachable inputs appropriate to the mode. No sampling bout starts at body temperature outside 7–34 or health below 20; those conditions also stop further samples. These are conservative activity guards, not a diagnosis or assurance of safety. Moss's supplied temperature is 30.92355458 and energy 40; use live values at execution, not the request's older approximate temperature.\\\\nMinimum executable mechanism: inspect the current occupied site and record which environmental thermal factors the existing simulation actually represents, the actor's observable temperature, local observable temperature if available, and any visible physical obstruction. At sample start and after each normal world round, record body temperature, available local environmental readings, location, weather/time context actually observable, and the environmental contribution to temperature change only where the engine can legitimately measure or derive it. Preserve distinctions among measured readings, visible geometry, model-derived protection and unknown properties. A temperature change alone is not evidence of shade or a controlled comparison. Do not expose internal stock, undiscovered object IDs, or other hidden state.\\\\nReuse the established thermal-update implementation exactly once per ordinary round; sampling must not add an extra cooling or warming update. In the development handoff, locate and record the exact thermal handler and revision used. If it already resolves spatial exposure or obstruction, reuse that resolver and record its exact reference. If no such mechanism exists, ship the occupied-location measurement precursor rather than inventing shade from names: shelter effectiveness stays unrepresented, and visits can identify only differences that the existing thermal model genuinely produces. A missing physical protection resolver remains an explicit development dependency for protection claims, not an excuse to label all shelters permanently impossible. No fixed-era unlock or unrelated construction roadmap is required.\\\\nComparison: consume no samples. Read exact personal records created by actual completed sampling rounds. Show locations, round numbers, measured changes, available environmental contributions and context differences; state insufficient evidence when a comparison lacks measurements or compatible conditions. Only 